==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|sociable}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|sociabilis}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# (of a person) Tending to [[socialize]] or be [[social]]
#: ''He's normally pretty quiet, but he gets much more '''sociable''' around women.''
#: {{syn|en|friendly|inviting|congenial}}
#* '''c. 1611''', {{William Shakespeare}}, ''Cymbeline''
#*: Society is no comfort to one not '''sociable'''.
# Offering opportunities for conversation; characterized by much conversation.
#: ''a '''sociable''' party''
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Capable of being, or fit to be, united in one body or company; [[associable]].
#* Hooker
#*: They are '''sociable''' parts united into one body.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} No longer hostile; friendly.
#: {{rfquotek|Beaumont and Fletcher}}

====Antonyms====
* {{sense|tending to socialize}} {{l|en|unsociable}}

